# tar — real-world examples

## Extract an uncompressed archive

```sh
tar -xvf backup.tar
```

## Create an uncompressed archive

```sh
tar -cvf backup.tar ~/documents/
```

## Extract a gzip archive

```sh
tar -xzvf backup.tgz
```

## Create a gzip archive

```sh
tar -czvf backup.tgz ~/documents/
```

## List contents without extracting

```sh
tar -ztvf backup.tgz
```

## Create a bzip2 archive (smaller, slower)

```sh
tar -cjvf backup.tar.bz2 ~/documents/
```

## Back up the web root, excluding media files

```sh
tar -czvf site.tgz --exclude='*.{jpg,gif,png,mkv,mp4,zip}' /srv/www/htdocs/
```

## Extract into a specific directory

```sh
tar -xzf backup.tgz -C /srv/restore/
```

## Archive only files matching a find

```sh
find . -name '*.conf' -mtime +0 -print | tar -czvf configs.tgz --files-from -
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | What it does                     |
| ---- | -------------------------------- |
| -c   | create an archive                |
| -x   | extract an archive               |
| -t   | list contents                    |
| -v   | verbose                          |
| -f   | archive file name (put it last)  |
| -z   | gzip                             |
| -j   | bzip2                            |
| -C   | change to directory before work  |
